        </details>
      </nav>
      <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The leaders of the G7, seven of the largest Western economies, agreed on October 2 to release 100 million barrels of oil from emergency stocks over four months, starting immediately, with what their statement calls "a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days." They also pledged not to restrict energy exports to one another; hours later President Donald Trump, who had been weighing a ban on U.S. diesel exports, said he would not impose one. The statement names no amount of diesel and no country that will supply it, and it ties the release to a pledge governments made in March, "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="The G7 requests the IEA to monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments. In this regard" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">In March, 32 members of the International Energy Agency (IEA), which coordinates the emergency oil stocks of industrialized countries, pledged to release 400 million barrels. By October 2 about 325 million had been released. More than 75 million is still owed, because more than 400 million was promised: the IEA's country-by-country list of March pledges, which includes extra oil production from Canada and Mexico, adds up to 426 million, and some countries released more than they promised. On October 7 the IEA said that releasing everything pledged and not yet released "would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market," and its member governments backed finishing the March release as soon as possible and putting diesel first. Germany's economy ministry said the 100 million barrels carry out the March obligations, and that "as of now, no additional quantities" would be released.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Total IEA,426.0307508005477" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Canada,23.631,0,0,23.631,23.631,0" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="with some countries having released greater volumes than they initially pledged" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="The full release of all the stocks that had been pledged but have not yet been released from the March 2026 Collective Action would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market." /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="IEA Member governments expressed support for accelerating the oil stock releases announced in the Collective Action of March 2026 with a view to completing them as soon as possible" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="IEA Members also supported the prioritisation of the release of diesel stocks" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Against this background, and taking into account commitments already fulfilled, the obligations are to be implemented through a coordinated release of 100 million barrels via the IEA." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The White House describes the deal differently: on October 5 it said President Donald Trump "successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel." It does not say whether any of that diesel is beyond what Europe pledged in March. Two people close to the matter said the IEA's governing board, which meets the week of October 12, is expected to decide who releases what. In the United States, retail diesel cost {prices.dieselOct5} dollars a gallon in the week of October 5, down from a record {prices.dieselSep21} on September 21 and up from {prices.dieselMar2} on March 2.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="In October 2026, President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="the next scheduled meeting of the IEA Governing Board, which will take place next week" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting, two people close to the matter said" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Mar 03/02 3.897" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The leaders also agreed to coordinate maintenance at their refineries so they do not shut at the same time, to run them harder "where feasible," to encourage countries with large refining capacity to make more diesel, and to "refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries." They call for the "immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency, which holds the G7 chair this year and convened the video call, published the same English statement, including the diesel release "within the first 20 days."<Cite s="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Attending the October 2 call were President Donald Trump, Chancellor Friedrich Merz, Prime Minister Mark Carney, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni, European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo, with the agency's executive director, Fatih Birol.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="The meeting was attended by President Donald Trump of the United States, Chancellor Friedrich Merz of Germany, Prime Minister Mark Carney of Canada, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy, President Ursula von der Leyen of the European Commission, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Miliband stood in for Prime Minister Andy Burnham, who attended his father's funeral. British pump prices had passed £2 a litre for the first time.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband will stand in for Andy Burnham during urgent G7 talks regarding global energy markets, as the Prime Minister attends his father’s funeral." /><Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" kind="record" detail>
          <p>IEA members still hold about 1.1 billion barrels of public emergency stocks, "including over 200 million barrels of diesel," and the IEA "stands ready to release more of these stocks to the market if and when required."<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="equivalent to around 1.1 billion barrels, including over 200 million barrels of diesel" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="The IEA stands ready to release more of these stocks to the market if and when required" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="whose-barrels">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Whose barrels</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Of the barrels pledged in March, the United States promised the largest share, {release.usMarch} barrels from its Strategic Petroleum Reserve, the government's emergency crude stock. The U.S. share is all crude oil; the diesel in the White House's account is Europe's. The Energy Department lends the oil to companies, which pay it back later with extra barrels. It lent {usShare.awarded} barrels between March 20 and June 22 and on September 29 offered up to {release.exchange} barrels more to cover the rest, with bids due October 6.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",United States,172.196,172.196,0,0,172.196,0" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="The first oil shipments began today" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-06-22" passage="As of June 22, 2026, the following contract for the exchange of 500,000 barrels has been awarded" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="October 6, 2026" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The March pledge and the U.S. share</strong><span>The first four rows are the IEA's figures; the last four are the Energy Department's, for the United States.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Measure", "Barrels"]} rows={ledgerRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" kind="computed" detail>
          <p>The department's five rounds of awards add up to {usShare.awarded} barrels, and the crude held in the reserve fell from {usShare.sprMar20} barrels on March 20 to {usShare.sprOct2} on October 2, a drop of {usShare.sprDrop}, close to the {usShare.awarded} awarded. The June offer of 40 million barrels drew one award, of 500,000 barrels. By May 11 the department said about 35 million barrels had been delivered.<Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="DOE will move forward with an exchange of 45.2 million barrels of crude oil and receive 55 million barrels in return" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-10" passage="announced awards of contracts for 8.5-million-barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-17" passage="exchange 26 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" passage="the contract awards for the exchange of approximately 53.3 million barrels of crude" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-06-22" passage="As of June 22, 2026, the following contract for the exchange of 500,000 barrels has been awarded" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" passage="To date, approximately 35 million barrels have been delivered to the market" /><Cite s="eia-spr-stocks-2026-10-07" passage="03/20 415,442" /><Cite s="eia-spr-stocks-2026-10-07" passage="2026-Oct 10/02 282,983" /></p>
          <DataTable columns={["Award", "Barrels"]} rows={awardRows} />
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The department says the earlier exchanges brought "a 25 percent premium in returned barrels." The September 29 release says "Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026"; the March, April and May awards began delivering as soon as they were made.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="achieving a 25 percent premium in returned barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-10" passage="with deliveries beginning immediately" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The reserve holds the least oil since 1982. U.S. law bars the president from ordering routine small drawdowns below 252.4 million barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="The amount of oil in US SPR has sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982." /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Washington says Europe is further behind. "While the United States and Japan are delivering on their commitments, several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged," Energy Secretary Chris Wright said on September 29. Germany had promised about 19.5 million barrels of oil, diesel and jet fuel and had released less than a quarter of it. Berlin puts that down to weak demand: on October 1 the German government wrote that companies had bought a first portion of the oil it offered and that the market saw no need for more. France told other governments privately it still had 8 million barrels to release, two diplomats said. Italy says it has met its pledge. Japan says it has been releasing oil from its reserves for some time and has no plans for a further release.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="While the United States and Japan are delivering on their commitments, several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Germany,19.510387406150176" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Of the 2.65 million tons of oil, diesel and jet fuel promised, Germany has so far released only 600,000 tons onto the market." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Germany attributes that to weak demand for the barrels, according to a document seen by POLITICO." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Paris has indicated privately that it still has 8 million barrels left under the March commitment" /><Cite s="reuters-italy-2026-10-08" passage="Italy has fully met its commitments under a March oil stocks release plan coordinated by the International Energy Agency" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">Germany's economy ministry ties the phrase to the March obligations: "taking into account commitments already fulfilled, the obligations are to be implemented through a coordinated release of 100 million barrels via the IEA." An internal European document seen by Euronews puts it the same way: "Because some members over-delivered, the 100 million barrels in the G7 statement can be covered within the existing action if everyone fulfils their commitments." It lists Greece, Italy and Portugal as finished, and France, Germany, the Netherlands and Spain as still assessing their stocks.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="taking into account commitments already fulfilled, the obligations are to be implemented through a coordinated release of 100 million barrels via the IEA" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Total IEA,426.0307508005477" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Total IEA,426.0307508005477" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Canada,23.631,0,0,23.631,23.631,0" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="with some countries having released greater volumes than they initially pledged" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="The full release of all the stocks that had been pledged but have not yet been released from the March 2026 Collective Action would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market." /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="Because some members over-delivered, the 100 million barrels in the G7 statement can be covered within the existing action if everyone fulfils their commitments" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="Greece, Italy and Portugal have met their pledged commitments. Emergency oil stocks from France, Germany, the Netherlands and Spain are under assessment." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The IEA's country table of March 19 lists pledges adding up to {marchTableRows[3][1]} barrels, including extra production that is not drawn from stocks, and says its split is "not final and are subject to change." Canada and Mexico counted extra production, not stocks. In that table France pledged 14.6 million barrels, Germany 19.5 million, Italy 10 million and Japan 79.8 million.<Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Total IEA,426.0307508005477,279.8429074061502,118.64364339439757,27.531" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage="Breakdown by public/obligated industry stocks and by crude/products are not final and are subject to change" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Canada,23.631,0,0,23.631,23.631,0" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",France,14.637961690000001" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Germany,19.510387406150176" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Italy,9.965968075999998" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",Japan,79.82" /></p>
          <DataTable columns={["March schedule", "Barrels"]} rows={marchTableRows} />
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In March the IEA said the release would "largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It was the sixth such joint action since the agency was founded in 1974.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The document counts 326 million barrels released. Portugal, it says, will consider new commitments only once every member has delivered, noting that about 50 million barrels remain pending at EU level. JPMorgan puts Europe's unreleased share at about 40 million of the 108 million it pledged.<Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="Of its 400 million barrels, 326 million have been released." /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="noting that about 50 million barrels remain pending at EU level, according to the document" /><Cite s="reuters-italy-2026-10-08" passage="According to JPMorgan, Europe still has around 40 million barrels left to release of 108 million barrels pledged." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Germany's emergency reserve covers about 90 days and holds about 20 million tonnes of crude and products; the IEA asked Germany to release 2.6 million tonnes.<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Deutschlands Notfallreserve für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="why-diesel">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why diesel is short</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The war that began when the United States and Israel attacked Iran in late February choked the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow sea lane out of the Persian Gulf, through which a fifth of the world's traded oil and gas passed before the war. The G7 leaders condemned Iran's attacks on its neighbors and its disruption of trade. By August the flow through the strait was 13.1 million barrels a day below its pre-war level, the IEA counts. Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates sent what oil they could around it, through Saudi Arabia's Red Sea port of Yanbu and the Emirati port of Fujairah, until attacks by Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi rebels cut those flows and, in early September, shut the pipeline to Yanbu. Fuel was hit harder than crude: in August the Gulf's diesel exports were about a quarter of their pre-war level, while its crude exports were just under 45 percent down.<Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="saw ship traffic stifled after the U.S. and Israel attacked Iran in late February" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We condemn Iran’s continued attacks against its regional neighbours and its disruption of international trade" /><Cite s="iea-commentary-2026-09-18" passage="Flows through the Strait of Hormuz averaged only 7.6 mb/d in August" /><Cite s="iea-commentary-2026-09-18" passage="That puts them 13.1 mb/d below pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-commentary-2026-09-18" passage="Exports from Saudi Arabia’s Red Sea port of Yanbu and the UAE’s Gulf of Oman port of Fujairah rose from 4.1 mb/d in February to a high of 7.8 mb/d in June" /><Cite s="iea-commentary-2026-09-18" passage="before Houthi attacks in the Red Sea cut those flows back to 5.5 mb/d in August" /><Cite s="iea-commentary-2026-09-18" passage="Attacks on the Saudi East-West pipeline that carries oil to Yanbu resulted in its shutdown in early September" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Crude losses appear to have narrowed to just below 45%" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="refined product and LPG exports remain nearly 60%, or 3.7 mb/d, less than in February" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="ap-iran-proposal-2026-09-26" passage="Yemen’s Iran-backed Houthi rebels" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Europe had lost a major source of diesel before the war began. Russia had supplied 53 percent of the diesel shipped into northwest Europe until the European Union banned seaborne imports of Russian petroleum products, including diesel, from February 5, 2023, and Europe turned to more distant suppliers. This year Ukrainian strikes on Russian refineries brought Russia's fuel exports to "a near-halt," the IEA says, and Russia extended its ban on diesel exports by its refiners to October 31. On October 1 four people briefed on the matter said China's refiners, which had exported 4.58 million tonnes of gasoline, diesel and jet fuel in August, had stopped fuel exports beyond Hong Kong and Macau until Beijing says otherwise.<Cite s="eia-eu-russian-diesel-2023-03-07" passage="banned seaborne imports of petroleum products from Russia, including diesel fuel (effective February 5, 2023)" /><Cite s="eia-eu-russian-diesel-2023-03-07" passage="diesel imports from Russia made up 53% of Northwest Europe’s seaborne imports" /><Cite s="eia-eu-russian-diesel-2023-03-07" passage="Europe’s loss of Russia as a significant diesel fuel supplier means it will need to import from more distant regions" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Disruptions to Russia’s refining system and a near-halt to product exports following intensified Ukrainian attacks have compounded these losses" /><Cite s="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" passage="The Russian government has extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel, ship fuel and gasoil for producers, which was set to expire today, until October 31" /><Cite s="reuters-china-exports-2026-10-01" passage="with gasoline, diesel and jet fuel exports in August totalling 4.58 million metric tons" /><Cite s="reuters-china-exports-2026-10-01" passage="Chinese refiners have suspended exports of oil products to regions beyond Hong Kong and Macau until further notice from Beijing, four people briefed on the matter said" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Fuel prices rose further than crude. In early September U.S. diesel was 94 percent above its pre-war price, the IEA said, while Brent, the benchmark crude, was 45 percent above.<Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="the rise in both crude futures and physical prices pales in comparison with those for refined products, where market tightness is now most acute" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="94% above pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="45% above pre-war levels" /></p>
        
        <SourcedBlock source="gasdesw-2026-10-03" kind="computed" detail>
          <figure class="story-figure">
          <figcaption><strong>Diesel and crude</strong><span>Weekly U.S. retail diesel in dollars a gallon; daily Brent and WTI spot prices in dollars a barrel.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Series", "Date", "Dollars"]} rows={priceRows} />
        </figure>
          <p>Retail diesel on October 5 was {prices.dieselFromMarchPercent} percent above March 2. Brent on September 29 was {prices.brentFromFebruaryPercent} percent above February 27, and WTI {prices.wtiFromFebruaryPercent} percent. Brent and WTI are daily spot prices and retail diesel a weekly price, not the IEA's pre-war averages. Brent futures settled at $102.25 on October 2 and $100.20 on October 7.<Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Mar 03/02 3.897" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-29,113.96" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,71.32" /><Cite s="dcoilwtico-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-29,96.16" /><Cite s="dcoilwtico-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,66.96" /><Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="Brent settled down 6 cents, or 0.06%, at $102.25 a barrel." /><Cite s="reuters-oil-settle-2026-10-07" passage="Brent crude futures settled down 38 cents, or 0.38%, at $100.20 a barrel." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Birol told the G7 leaders that "refined-product flows remain severely constrained, with Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries exacerbating the situation for diesel."<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="refined-product flows remain severely constrained, with Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries exacerbating the situation for diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Russia's government said its ban keeps the domestic fuel market stable, "including amid heightened demand for motor fuel during the harvest." On October 2 Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said Russia would consider partly lifting the ban in case of overproduction.<Cite s="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" passage="The decision was made to maintain a stable situation on the domestic fuel market, including amid heightened demand for motor fuel during the harvest" /><Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="Russia said on Friday it would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-china-exports-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Beijing restricted fuel exports in March after the war began and eased the curbs from July to September.<Cite s="reuters-china-exports-2026-10-01" passage="Beijing restricted fuel exports in March following the outbreak of the U.S.-Israeli war on Iran" /><Cite s="reuters-china-exports-2026-10-01" passage="before relaxing those curbs between July and September" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="centcom-blockade-2026-07-13" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The U.S. military has blockaded ships going to and from Iranian ports since July 14, after an earlier blockade from April 13 to June 18. Iran has offered to reopen Hormuz within seven days if the United States lifts the blockade, waives sanctions on Iranian oil and observes a ceasefire that includes Lebanon.<Cite s="centcom-blockade-2026-07-13" passage="will resume blockading maritime traffic entering and exiting Iranian ports on July 14" /><Cite s="centcom-blockade-2026-07-13" passage="follows the initial implementation from April 13 to June 18" /><Cite s="ap-iran-proposal-2026-09-26" passage="Iran has offered to reopen the Strait of Hormuz and resume talks on its nuclear program in seven days if the United States lifts its naval blockade of Iranian ports, waives sanctions on Iranian oil sales and observes a ceasefire that would include Lebanon." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Washington and Europe</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">France called the G7 meeting. On September 18 President Emmanuel Macron, whose country holds the G7 chair, said he would convene the G7 on stock levels and "considérer les options de libération potentielle de stocks stratégiques," the possible release of strategic stocks. He blamed record pump prices on the wars, on a Strait of Hormuz "quasiment bloqué," nearly blocked, and on Houthi attacks that had cut the flows through Yanbu.<Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="je convoquerai un G7 pour avancer sur une meilleure coordination sur les niveaux de stock" /><Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="pour aussi considérer les options de libération potentielle de stocks stratégiques ou de déblocage" /><Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="Le détroit d'Ormuz reste quasiment bloqué." /><Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="après les attaques des houthis sur Yanbou, ces volumes ont aussi fortement diminué" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Washington pushed harder. The United States supplied about half of the EU's diesel imports in August, the IEA said. With U.S. retail diesel at a record {prices.dieselSep21} dollars a gallon in the week of September 21, Trump said on September 30 he was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports, though he added that a ban "might raise the price of other things." On October 1 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent wrote that "our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available," and that American farmers, truckers and businesses "should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage."<Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`still "thinking about" banning diesel exports`} /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="but it might raise the price of other things." /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EU trade chief Maroš Šefčovič said a ban would have "dramatic consequences for our economic performance." "We fully reject any ban on diesel. A ban would not be beneficial to anyone," European Commission spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said on October 2; it "would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." After the G7 call, EU officials agreed to release no more than the March barrels for now, "pending a clearer IEA assessment of market conditions and security-of-supply risks," one diplomat said; some countries warned that releasing stocks now could leave them exposed in a supply crisis. After the call, Commission President Ursula von der Leyen welcomed "the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies."<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="dramatic consequences for our economic performance" /><Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel. A ban would not be beneficial to anyone" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." /><Cite s="politico-eu-iea-assessment-2026-10-07" passage="pending a clearer IEA assessment of market conditions and security-of-supply risks" /><Cite s="politico-eu-iea-assessment-2026-10-07" passage="some EU countries warned that releasing stocks now could leave them vulnerable in the event of a supply crisis" /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Two House bills introduced on September 16 by Representatives Tim Burchett and Clay Fuller would ban diesel exports: H.R. 10422 from the 14th straight day the average retail price is above $5 a gallon until the first day after 30 straight days below $4.50, and H.R. 10423 through December 31, 2026. Both were referred to the Foreign Affairs Committee.<Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="Secretary of Commerce shall prohibit the export of diesel fuel" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="begins on the date that is the fourteenth consecutive" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="greater than $5 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="thirtieth consecutive day on which" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="less than $4.50 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited during the period beginning on the date of the enactment of this subsection and ending on the date December 31, 2026." /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="which was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 22 Trump said, "I've said let's not send out the diesel." Several Senate Republicans had called for a ban, an RBC note says, and industry groups wrote to Trump against one.<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /><Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban" /><Cite s="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" passage="have sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits of diesels exports." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Germany's government welcomed the G7 decision as a sign of the group's unity.<Cite s="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" passage="Auch die Bundesregierung hat den G-7-Beschluss zur Freigabe von 100 Millionen Barrel Öl und Ölprodukten wie Diesel als wichtiges Zeichen für die Handlungsfähigkeit und Geschlossenheit der Gruppe begrüßt." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Germany and Japan say no more; France and Italy leave it open</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">Germany and Japan say they will release no more for now. France signaled it could release 2 million barrels of diesel on top of its March share but did not commit, two diplomats said, and Italy, which says it has met its March pledge, is open to providing more once other European countries meet theirs. France, Germany, the Netherlands and Spain are still assessing their stocks, the European document says. JPMorgan's analysts, reading the G7 statement, wrote that "the headline 100 million barrels does not represent 100 million barrels of new intervention."<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="there are no plans for a further release at this stage" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="France on Wednesday signaled it could release 2 million barrels of diesel on top of the quantities agreed in March" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="Emergency oil stocks from France, Germany, the Netherlands and Spain are under assessment." /><Cite s="reuters-italy-2026-10-08" passage="is open to providing more once other European countries meet theirs" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-march-volumes-2026-10-07" passage="Our interpretation of the press release is that the headline 100 million barrels does not represent 100 million barrels of new intervention" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Diesel, or diesel and crude</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The G7 statement promises a diesel release in the first 20 days and gives no split between diesel and crude; the French presidency's readout says refined products, including diesel, and crude. Trump, Wright and the White House described it as diesel, though in March the IEA said Europe's contributions would be mostly refined products and the overall release mostly crude. Trump posted that Europe "has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil." Wright called the release "the majority diesel." The White House said "100 million barrels of refined diesel."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="the majority diesel" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="100 million barrels of refined diesel" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /></p>

            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>What Washington asked of Europe, and the denials</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Three people close to the talks said on October 1 that the administration had told Germany and France to draw down their diesel stocks "or face a potential US diesel export ban." The sources are unnamed, and their accounts of the demand differ: one said Washington asked the EU for 120 million barrels of diesel over six months, another that it demanded 100 million barrels of diesel within 20 days from France, Germany and other large European countries. One European diplomat called it "blackmail." Two days after saying he was still thinking about a ban, and hours after the G7 agreed, Trump ruled one out: "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing." Macron said Trump did not threaten an embargo and "was particularly clear" that there would be no export bans.<Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="The Trump administration has told Germany and France to draw down emergency diesel inventories to help to ease global fuel prices or face a potential US diesel export ban, said three people close to the discussions." /><Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="The US had demanded large European countries including France and Germany release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window, one of the sources told Reuters on Friday." /><Cite s="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" passage="One European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail,” an official briefed on a call between EU energy officials early Friday told POLITICO." /><Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="President Trump was particularly clear on this point" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
        <SourcedBlock source="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>On October 2 Eric Daugherty, chief content officer of the Florida's Voice and Right Line News sites, who has 1.2 million followers on X, posted that "100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins." In a television interview that day Rollins, the agriculture secretary, spoke of "100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon" and said "I think they’re moving out right now," without calling them diesel. France's 10 million barrels go to French distributors.<Cite s="daugherty-profile-2026-10-08" passage="Chief Content Officer @RightLineNews @FLVoiceNews" /><Cite s="daugherty-profile-2026-10-08" passage="Followers: 1205360" /><Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon." /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="I think they’re moving out right now." /><Cite s="reuters-france-10m-2026-10-07" passage="available to distributors in France" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>How each account described the 100 million barrels.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="the majority diesel" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="100 million barrels of refined diesel" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /></p>
          <DataTable columns={["Who", "What they said the 100 million barrels are"]} rows={statementRows} />
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Before the call, EU governments discussed a French proposal for European countries to release 50 million barrels of diesel and IEA members 50 million barrels of crude, and said any deal should include a U.S. commitment not to ban diesel exports, one person familiar with the discussion said.<Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="any agreement on further diesel stock releases should include a US commitment to avoid a unilateral diesel export ban" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas, an energy columnist at Bloomberg, wrote that whether the release includes March barrels is "a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now."<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Energy and commodities columnist at Bloomberg." /><Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="It's a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The IEA's governing board meets the week of October 12, and the G7 asked the IEA for a follow-up report "before 20 days," with recommendations, including on refilling stocks. Russell Hardy, chief executive of the commodities trading house Vitol, said the next two weeks would show "exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out." On October 7 France said it would sell 10 million barrels of diesel from its reserves to French distributors at the price it paid before the crisis. The government estimates that will cut diesel pump prices by 12 to 18 euro cents a litre for three months. It did not say whether the 10 million include the 8 million it still owed from March.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="the next scheduled meeting of the IEA Governing Board, which will take place next week" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days, include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="over the next two weeks, we'll see tenders coming out from different bodies, and those tenders will tell us exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out." /><Cite s="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" passage="le Gouvernement annonce ce jour la mise sur le marché de 10 millions de barils de diesel" /><Cite s="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" passage="Ils seront mis à disposition des distributeurs à leur prix d'acquisition avant crise" /><Cite s="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" passage="dans un ordre de grandeur évalué entre 12 et 18 centimes par litre de gazole, et ce pendant trois mois" /><Cite s="reuters-france-10m-2026-10-07" passage="will automatically lower prices at the pump by an estimated amount ranging from 0.12 to 0.18 euros per liter of diesel" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Bids for this solicitation are due no later than 11:00 A.M. Central Time on Tuesday, October 6, 2026." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Oil prices rose again as supply from the Gulf worsened. On October 6 only seven commodity ships crossed Hormuz, the fewest since July 23, after the heaviest week of attacks on tankers since the war began. More crude went around the strait instead: exports from the Gulf of Oman coast and the Red Sea rose to more than twice their pre-war level; overall, the region's crude exports that week were back at their pre-war level, the analytics firm Kpler counted. On October 8 oil prices settled 4 percent higher on worries about the war and a hurricane approaching the U.S. Gulf Coast, where producers had stopped 62.9 percent of their oil output; Brent crude futures rose from $100.20 to $104.28 a barrel. Iran has offered to reopen Hormuz within seven days if the United States lifts the naval blockade of Iranian ports it has enforced since July 14, waives sanctions on Iranian oil sales and observes a ceasefire that includes Lebanon. On October 8 Trump said talks were "productive" and that the United States would not attack Iran before the November 3 elections.<Cite s="reuters-hormuz-transits-2026-10-08" passage="Seven commodity vessels passed through the strait on Tuesday, for the lowest figure since July 23, data from analytics firm Kpler showed." /><Cite s="reuters-hormuz-transits-2026-10-08" passage="after attacks on tankers in the key waterway reached their highest last week since the start of the US-Israeli war with Iran" /><Cite s="reuters-hormuz-transits-2026-10-08" passage="exports from the Gulf of Oman coast and the Red Sea rose to 6.7 million bpd, more than twice pre-war levels" /><Cite s="reuters-hormuz-transits-2026-10-08" passage="This helped keep overall exports of Middle East crude at the pre-war level, they said." /><Cite s="reuters-oil-jumps-2026-10-08" passage="Oil prices settled 4% higher on Thursday on revived concerns about the ongoing war in the Middle East and growing supply disruptions due to a hurricane approaching the US Gulf Coast" /><Cite s="reuters-oil-jumps-2026-10-08" passage="have shut in about 1.3 million barrels per day, or 62.9%, of current oil production as of Thursday" /><Cite s="reuters-oil-jumps-2026-10-08" passage="Brent crude futures closed up $4.08, or 4.1%, at $104.28" /><Cite s="reuters-oil-settle-2026-10-07" passage="Brent crude futures settled down 38 cents, or 0.38%, at $100.20 a barrel." /><Cite s="ap-iran-proposal-2026-09-26" passage="Iran has offered to reopen the Strait of Hormuz and resume talks on its nuclear program in seven days if the United States lifts its naval blockade of Iranian ports, waives sanctions on Iranian oil sales and observes a ceasefire that would include Lebanon." /><Cite s="centcom-blockade-2026-07-13" passage="will resume blockading maritime traffic entering and exiting Iranian ports on July 14" /><Cite s="reuters-trump-iran-2026-10-08" passage="We are having productive discussions with the Islamic Republic of Iran" /><Cite s="reuters-trump-iran-2026-10-08" passage="We will not be attacking Iran at any time prior to the midterm elections to be held in the United States on November 3rd." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" kind="record" detail>
          <p>SAGESS, the company that manages France's strategic stocks, will sell the barrels, and the government says it will check every week that the cut reaches the pump. A government spokeswoman said the 10 million barrels are about a fifth of the 54 million barrels of diesel France uses in three months.<Cite s="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" passage="Les barils seront mis sur le marché par la SAGESS" /><Cite s="france-finance-diesel-2026-10-07" passage="Nous contrôlerons chaque semaine que cette baisse arrive à la pompe" /><Cite s="reuters-italy-2026-10-08" passage="the release would cover about 20% of the country's diesel consumption of 54 million barrels over a three-month period" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Euronews dates the board meeting October 15 and says it will settle "volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released." Reuters's sources put it on October 14 and 15. Poland is ready to release if governments agree, its energy minister said.<Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="when the IEA Governing Board gathers governments to settle the details of the operation, including volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-march-volumes-2026-10-07" passage="Polish Energy Minister Milosz Motyka said on Wednesday that Poland was ready to release oil and diesel stocks if a consensus is reached" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The G7 will meet through the IEA "to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." The statement does not say 20 days from when.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will convene in the context of the IEA in the coming days to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 5 Trump signed an order saying "restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices." It tells the Treasury secretary to have the IRS announce, within five days, that it will not penalize highway use of off-road "dyed" diesel, the untaxed fuel sold for farms and building sites, from October 5 to December 31, and to defer specified diesel excise taxes for that period if he determines the relief is authorized.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="Restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="Today, President Donald J. Trump signed an Executive Order to temporarily allow off-road “dyed” diesel for highway use and defer the applicable Federal excise tax" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="Within 5 days of the date of this order, the Secretary shall direct the Internal Revenue Service to announce that it will not impose a penalty" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="when dyed diesel fuel is sold for use or used on the highway during the period of October 5, 2026, through December 31, 2026" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="If the Secretary makes those determinations, the Secretary shall, to the extent authorized by law, defer payment by those taxpayers of the taxes imposed by 26 U.S.C. 4041(a)(1)(A) or by 26 U.S.C. 4041(b)(1)(B)" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 2 U.S. oil prices fell as much as 5 percent at one point; Brent settled at $102.25 and WTI at $91.11. On October 7, after the IEA's meeting, Brent futures settled at $100.20 and WTI at $88.28.<Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /><Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="Brent settled down 6 cents, or 0.06%, at $102.25 a barrel." /><Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="WTI finished down $1.76, or 1.90%, at $91.11 a barrel." /><Cite s="reuters-oil-settle-2026-10-07" passage="Brent crude futures settled down 38 cents, or 0.38%, at $100.20 a barrel." /><Cite s="reuters-oil-settle-2026-10-07" passage="to $88.28." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
